{:edb/db-before-t 1946 :edb/db-after-t 1947 :edb/tx-data [[13194139535259 50 #inst "2026-10-08T20:37:01.465Z" 13194139535259 true] [17592186124367 1020 #edb/ref 17592186340356 13194139535259 true] [17592186124367 1020 #edb/ref 17592186340363 13194139535259 true] [17592186124368 1020 #edb/ref 17592186340370 13194139535259 true] [17592186124368 1020 #edb/ref 17592186340377 13194139535259 true] [17592186124368 1020 #edb/ref 17592186340384 13194139535259 true] [17592186340356 1017 #edb/ref 17592186340357 13194139535259 true] [17592186340356 1089 "q-133373" 13194139535259 true] [17592186340356 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045463 13194139535259 true] [17592186340356 1100 "Given that the function $f(x,y)$ and all of its higher-order partial derivatives are continuous on an open set $U,$ evaluate ${f}_{yxy}(6,\\frac{\\pi }{3})$ if ${f}_{xy}(x,y)=7+x\\operatorname{sin}⁡y.$" 13194139535259 true] [17592186340356 1113 #uuid "37ec63d6-7b9e-5cc5-b3cc-774fccb68297" 13194139535259 true] [17592186340356 1145 "As the higher-order partial derivatives are continuous on an open set $U,$ the mixed partial derivatives must be equal. Hence, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{yxy}(x,y) & ={f}_{xyy}(x,y) \\\\  & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}({f}_{xy}(x,y)) \\\\  & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(7+x\\operatorname{sin}⁡y) \\\\  & =x\\operatorname{cos}⁡y.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we evaluate the partial derivative at the point $(6,\\frac{\\pi }{3}):$\n\n$$\n{f}_{yxy}(6,\\frac{\\pi }{3})=6\\cdot \\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{3})=3\n$$" 13194139535259 true] [17592186340357 1000 #edb/ref 17592186340358 13194139535259 true] [17592186340357 1000 #edb/ref 17592186340359 13194139535259 true] [17592186340357 1000 #edb/ref 17592186340360 13194139535259 true] [17592186340357 1000 #edb/ref 17592186340361 13194139535259 true] [17592186340357 1000 #edb/ref 17592186340362 13194139535259 true] [17592186340357 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045450 13194139535259 true] [17592186340357 1041 "selection" 13194139535259 true] [17592186340357 1104 #edb/ref 17592186340358 13194139535259 true] [17592186340357 1110 #uuid "4e20f6bb-bccd-5986-afdc-f8f5c8c5c714" 13194139535259 true] [17592186340358 1034 "3" 13194139535259 true] [17592186340358 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139535259 true] [17592186340358 1111 #uuid "69b606c9-0e08-5d03-979e-ed29c0f0169b" 13194139535259 true] [17592186340359 1034 "\\frac{1}{2}" 13194139535259 true] [17592186340359 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139535259 true] [17592186340359 1111 #uuid "e0277310-9c11-570c-a604-eeded6955db4" 13194139535259 true] [17592186340360 1034 "6" 13194139535259 true] [17592186340360 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139535259 true] [17592186340360 1111 #uuid "04f5b191-6c29-5678-99dc-f9e7a2671d35" 13194139535259 true] [17592186340361 1034 "\\frac{1}{3}" 13194139535259 true] [17592186340361 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139535259 true] [17592186340361 1111 #uuid "e48ae0d7-e2a3-54ef-8912-2be0952dc224" 13194139535259 true] [17592186340362 1034 "\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}" 13194139535259 true] [17592186340362 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139535259 true] [17592186340362 1111 #uuid "53bf4a48-ada8-5510-971e-c00135eeef3f" 13194139535259 true] [17592186340363 1017 #edb/ref 17592186340364 13194139535259 true] [17592186340363 1089 "q-36559" 13194139535259 true] [17592186340363 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045464 13194139535259 true] [17592186340363 1100 "Suppose that a function $f(x,y,z)$ and all of its higher-order partial derivatives are continuous on an open set $U,$ and that\n\n$$\n\\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂y∂z}=3{y}^{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(xz)+3x{y}^{2}z\\operatorname{cos}⁡(xz)+3{x}^{2}{z}^{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(xy)\n$$\n\nEvaluate the following expression at the point $(\\pi ,-1,1):$\n\n$$\n\\frac{{∂}^{3}f}{∂z∂y∂y}+\\frac{{∂}^{3}f}{∂y∂z∂z}\n$$" 13194139535259 true] [17592186340363 1113 #uuid "8f246fa5-d146-527a-80ba-21dffbe00cb8" 13194139535259 true] [17592186340363 1145 "As the higher order partial derivatives are continuous on an open set $U,$ the mixed partial derivatives must be equal. \n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{∂}^{3}f}{∂z∂y∂y} & =\\frac{{∂}^{3}f}{∂y∂y∂z} \\\\  & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(\\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂y∂z}) \\\\  & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(3{y}^{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(xz)+3x{y}^{2}z\\operatorname{cos}⁡(xz)+3{x}^{2}{z}^{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(xy)) \\\\  & =6y\\operatorname{sin}⁡(xz)+6xyz\\operatorname{cos}⁡(xz)-3{x}^{3}{z}^{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(xy).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn addition, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{∂}^{3}f}{∂y∂z∂z} & =\\frac{{∂}^{3}f}{∂z∂y∂z} \\\\  & =\\frac{∂}{∂z}(\\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂y∂z}) \\\\  & =\\frac{∂}{∂z}(3{y}^{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(xz)+3x{y}^{2}z\\operatorname{cos}⁡(xz)+3{x}^{2}{z}^{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(xy)) \\\\  & =3x{y}^{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(xz)+3x{y}^{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(xz)-3{x}^{2}{y}^{2}z\\operatorname{sin}⁡(xz)+6{x}^{2}z\\operatorname{cos}⁡(xy).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we evaluate both partial derivatives at the point $(\\pi ,-1,1):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{∂}^{3}f}{∂z∂y∂y}{|}_{(\\pi ,-1,1)} & =6(-1)\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\pi )+6(\\pi )(-1)(1)\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\pi )-3(\\pi )^{3}(1)^{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(-\\pi )=6\\pi  \\\\ \\frac{{∂}^{3}f}{∂y∂z∂z}{|}_{(\\pi ,-1,1)} & =3(\\pi )(-1)^{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\pi )+3(\\pi )(-1)^{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\pi )-3(\\pi )^{2}(-1)^{2}(1)\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\pi )+6(\\pi )^{2}(1)\\operatorname{cos}⁡(-\\pi )=-6\\pi -6{\\pi }^{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{∂}^{3}f}{∂z∂y∂y}{|}_{(\\pi ,-1,1)}+\\frac{{∂}^{3}f}{∂y∂z∂z}{|}_{(\\pi ,-1,1)} & =6\\pi +(-6\\pi -6{\\pi }^{2})=-6{\\pi }^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535259 true] [17592186340364 1000 #edb/ref 17592186340365 13194139535259 true] [17592186340364 1000 #edb/ref 17592186340366 13194139535259 true] [17592186340364 1000 #edb/ref 17592186340367 13194139535259 true] [17592186340364 1000 #edb/ref 17592186340368 13194139535259 true] [17592186340364 1000 #edb/ref 17592186340369 13194139535259 true] [17592186340364 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045450 13194139535259 true] [17592186340364 1041 "selection" 13194139535259 true] [17592186340364 1104 #edb/ref 17592186340369 13194139535259 true] [17592186340364 1110 #uuid "e7223cee-7059-5272-8855-3a5d62726deb" 13194139535259 true] [17592186340365 1034 "-12\\pi +6{\\pi }^{2}" 13194139535259 true] [17592186340365 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139535259 true] [17592186340365 1111 #uuid "a2331820-11ea-55ae-88cb-bd07dfd447cf" 13194139535259 true] [17592186340366 1034 "3{\\pi }^{3}-6{\\pi }^{2}" 13194139535259 true] [17592186340366 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139535259 true] [17592186340366 1111 #uuid "516cf784-06ce-5301-a1ea-3a49c22759f2" 13194139535259 true] [17592186340367 1034 "-3{\\pi }^{3}-6{\\pi }^{2}" 13194139535259 true] [17592186340367 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139535259 true] [17592186340367 1111 #uuid "daaa23d7-6a82-5836-9e81-4c88515da490" 13194139535259 true] [17592186340368 1034 "12\\pi -6{\\pi }^{2}" 13194139535259 true] [17592186340368 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139535259 true] [17592186340368 1111 #uuid "2505a76c-9b64-53f9-93a4-bb4a2f92a451" 13194139535259 true] [17592186340369 1034 "-6{\\pi }^{2}" 13194139535259 true] [17592186340369 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139535259 true] [17592186340369 1111 #uuid "88dad69f-75f8-564b-9a7c-095bb57ae1ee" 13194139535259 true] [17592186340370 1017 #edb/ref 17592186340371 13194139535259 true] [17592186340370 1089 "q-80652" 13194139535259 true] [17592186340370 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045465 13194139535259 true] [17592186340370 1100 "Suppose that the function $f(x,y)$ and all of its first and second-order partial derivatives are continuous on an open set $U.$ Which of the following pairs could represent the first-order partial derivatives of $f(x,y)?$\n\n- ${f}_{x}(x,y)=4x+{y}^{2}$ and ${f}_{y}(x,y)=4xy-y$\n- ${f}_{x}(x,y)=2x+y$ and ${f}_{y}(x,y)=x+\\sqrt{y}$\n- ${f}_{x}(x,y)=2xy+{y}^{2}$ and ${f}_{y}(x,y)={x}^{2}y+2x$" 13194139535259 true] [17592186340370 1113 #uuid "24df999b-6ea6-5095-ada6-d510b0d36076" 13194139535259 true] [17592186340370 1145 "Since the function and its first and second-order partial derivatives are continuous on an open set $U,$ the mixed partial derivatives must be equal, that is, ${f}_{xy}(x,y)={f}_{yx}(x,y).$ Let's check if this condition is true for each of our pairs of first-order partial derivatives.\n\n- For the first pair, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{xy}(x,y) & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(4x+{y}^{2})=2y \\\\ {f}_{yx}(x,y) & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(4xy-y)=4y.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAs ${f}_{xy}(x,y)\\ne {f}_{yx}(x,y)$, the first pair cannot represent the first-order partial derivatives of $f(x,y).$\n\n- For the second pair, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{xy}(x,y) & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(2x+y)=1 \\\\ {f}_{yx}(x,y) & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(x+\\sqrt{y})=1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAs ${f}_{xy}(x,y)={f}_{yx}(x,y)$, the second pair could represent the first-order partial derivatives of $f(x,y).$\n\n- For the third pair, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{xy}(x,y) & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(2xy+{y}^{2})=2x+2y \\\\ {f}_{yx}(x,y) & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}({x}^{2}y+2x)=2xy+2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAs ${f}_{xy}(x,y)\\ne {f}_{yx}(x,y)$,  the third pair cannot represent the first-order partial derivatives of $f(x,y).$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is 'II only'." 13194139535259 true] [17592186340371 1000 #edb/ref 17592186340372 13194139535259 true] [17592186340371 1000 #edb/ref 17592186340373 13194139535259 true] [17592186340371 1000 #edb/ref 17592186340374 13194139535259 true] [17592186340371 1000 #edb/ref 17592186340375 13194139535259 true] [17592186340371 1000 #edb/ref 17592186340376 13194139535259 true] [17592186340371 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045450 13194139535259 true] [17592186340371 1041 "selection" 13194139535259 true] [17592186340371 1104 #edb/ref 17592186340376 13194139535259 true] [17592186340371 1110 #uuid "ff9459e8-8590-5381-b76e-8e4952daa8ac" 13194139535259 true] [17592186340372 1034 "II and III only" 13194139535259 true] [17592186340372 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139535259 true] [17592186340372 1111 #uuid "97f935ac-dc65-581a-8690-483db1565e10" 13194139535259 true] [17592186340373 1034 "I only" 13194139535259 true] [17592186340373 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139535259 true] [17592186340373 1111 #uuid "bcbdae4c-ce01-5d34-aae9-6e924fc99290" 13194139535259 true] [17592186340374 1034 "III only" 13194139535259 true] [17592186340374 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139535259 true] [17592186340374 1111 #uuid "fdd65fbd-e573-541c-83cb-336af378b312" 13194139535259 true] [17592186340375 1034 "I and II only" 13194139535259 true] [17592186340375 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139535259 true] [17592186340375 1111 #uuid "cf78341f-bfae-5945-bdc7-43b516000b99" 13194139535259 true] [17592186340376 1034 "II only" 13194139535259 true] [17592186340376 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139535259 true] [17592186340376 1111 #uuid "a0a7b7d5-564f-5ed1-abd4-652d463d486d" 13194139535259 true] [17592186340377 1017 #edb/ref 17592186340378 13194139535259 true] [17592186340377 1089 "q-80759" 13194139535259 true] [17592186340377 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045464 13194139535259 true] [17592186340377 1100 "Suppose that the function $f(x,y)$ and all of its first and second-order partial derivatives are continuous on an open set $U.$ Which of the following pairs could represent the first-order partial derivatives of $f(x,y)?$\n\n- ${f}_{x}(x,y)={e}^{-2x}-y$ and ${f}_{y}(x,y)={e}^{-y}-2x$\n- ${f}_{x}(x,y)={e}^{x}\\operatorname{cos}⁡x$ and ${f}_{y}(x,y)=5$\n- ${f}_{x}(x,y)=2{y}^{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡xy+{y}^{2}$ and ${f}_{y}(x,y)=2\\operatorname{sin}⁡xy+2xy\\operatorname{cos}⁡xy+2xy$" 13194139535259 true] [17592186340377 1113 #uuid "57d651ac-3b25-5475-a085-e67636d23e90" 13194139535259 true] [17592186340377 1145 "Since the function and its first and second-order partial derivatives are continuous on an open set $U,$ the mixed partial derivatives must be equal, that is, ${f}_{xy}(x,y)={f}_{yx}(x,y).$ Let's check if this condition is true for each of our pairs of first-order partial derivatives.\n\n- For the first pair, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{xy}(x,y) & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}({e}^{-2x}-y)=-1 \\\\ {f}_{yx}(x,y) & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}({e}^{-y}-2x)=-2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAs ${f}_{xy}(x,y)\\ne {f}_{yx}(x,y)$, the first pair cannot represent the first-order partial derivatives of $f(x,y).$\n\n- For the second pair, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{xy}(x,y) & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}({e}^{x}\\operatorname{cos}⁡x)=0 \\\\ {f}_{yx}(x,y) & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(5)=0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAs ${f}_{xy}(x,y)={f}_{yx}(x,y)$, the second pair could represent the first-order partial derivatives of $f(x,y).$\n\n- For the third pair, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{xy}(x,y) & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(2{y}^{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡xy+{y}^{2})=4y\\operatorname{cos}⁡xy-2x{y}^{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡xy+2y \\\\ {f}_{yx}(x,y) & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(2\\operatorname{sin}⁡xy+2xy\\operatorname{cos}⁡xy+2xy) \\\\  & =2y\\operatorname{cos}⁡xy+2y\\operatorname{cos}⁡xy-2x{y}^{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡xy+2y \\\\  & =4y\\operatorname{cos}⁡xy-2x{y}^{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡xy+2y.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAs ${f}_{xy}(x,y)={f}_{yx}(x,y)$,  the third pair could represent the first-order partial derivatives of $f(x,y).$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is 'II and III only'." 13194139535259 true] [17592186340378 1000 #edb/ref 17592186340379 13194139535259 true] [17592186340378 1000 #edb/ref 17592186340380 13194139535259 true] [17592186340378 1000 #edb/ref 17592186340381 13194139535259 true] [17592186340378 1000 #edb/ref 17592186340382 13194139535259 true] [17592186340378 1000 #edb/ref 17592186340383 13194139535259 true] [17592186340378 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045450 13194139535259 true] [17592186340378 1041 "selection" 13194139535259 true] [17592186340378 1104 #edb/ref 17592186340379 13194139535259 true] [17592186340378 1110 #uuid "a500767d-0118-5c50-971c-18ecc5932da8" 13194139535259 true] [17592186340379 1034 "II and III only" 13194139535259 true] [17592186340379 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139535259 true] [17592186340379 1111 #uuid "dc93e348-c255-5eb5-84cc-29131719c035" 13194139535259 true] [17592186340380 1034 "III only" 13194139535259 true] [17592186340380 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139535259 true] [17592186340380 1111 #uuid "2274cd41-3523-5a35-9d10-a95f1b5d68b9" 13194139535259 true] [17592186340381 1034 "I and II only" 13194139535259 true] [17592186340381 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139535259 true] [17592186340381 1111 #uuid "922488ec-8953-5175-a96b-127f4cee91db" 13194139535259 true] [17592186340382 1034 "I, II and III" 13194139535259 true] [17592186340382 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139535259 true] [17592186340382 1111 #uuid "ddefbf88-4721-55c3-adae-d4153e8655f1" 13194139535259 true] [17592186340383 1034 "II only" 13194139535259 true] [17592186340383 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139535259 true] [17592186340383 1111 #uuid "24f7aa4e-f60b-5b4c-a54a-ae8a9f37919b" 13194139535259 true] [17592186340384 1017 #edb/ref 17592186340385 13194139535259 true] [17592186340384 1089 "q-80762" 13194139535259 true] [17592186340384 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045465 13194139535259 true] [17592186340384 1100 "Suppose that the function $f(x,y)$ and all of its first and second-order partial derivatives are continuous on an open set $U.$ Which of the following pairs could represent the first-order partial derivatives of $f(x,y)?$\n\n- ${f}_{x}(x,y)=2x-2{y}^{2}$ and ${f}_{y}(x,y)=2y-2xy$\n- ${f}_{x}(x,y)=\\operatorname{sin}⁡({e}^{x})$ and ${f}_{y}(x,y)={e}^{y}\\operatorname{cos}⁡y$\n- ${f}_{x}(x,y)=3{x}^{2}{y}^{2}$ and ${f}_{y}(x,y)=2{x}^{3}y-\\sqrt{y}$" 13194139535259 true] [17592186340384 1113 #uuid "813fc330-f1d0-5e6c-9e4f-df4f6cf7d2b1" 13194139535259 true] [17592186340384 1145 "Since the function and its first and second-order partial derivatives are continuous on an open set $U,$ the mixed partial derivatives must be equal, that is, ${f}_{xy}(x,y)={f}_{yx}(x,y).$ Let's check if this condition is true for each of our pairs of first-order partial derivatives.\n\n- For the first pair, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{xy}(x,y) & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(2x-2{y}^{2})=-4y \\\\ {f}_{yx}(x,y) & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(2y-2xy)=-2y.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAs ${f}_{xy}(x,y)\\ne {f}_{yx}(x,y)$, the first pair cannot represent the first-order partial derivatives of $f(x,y).$\n\n- For the second pair, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{xy}(x,y) & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡({e}^{x}))=0 \\\\ {f}_{yx}(x,y) & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}({e}^{y}\\operatorname{cos}⁡y)=0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAs ${f}_{xy}(x,y)={f}_{yx}(x,y)$, the second pair could represent the first-order partial derivatives of $f(x,y).$\n\n- For the third pair, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{xy}(x,y) & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(3{x}^{2}{y}^{2})=6{x}^{2}y \\\\ {f}_{yx}(x,y) & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(2{x}^{3}y-\\sqrt{y})=6{x}^{2}y.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAs ${f}_{xy}(x,y)={f}_{yx}(x,y)$,  the third pair could represent the first-order partial derivatives of $f(x,y).$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is 'II and III only'." 13194139535259 true] [17592186340385 1000 #edb/ref 17592186340386 13194139535259 true] [17592186340385 1000 #edb/ref 17592186340387 13194139535259 true] [17592186340385 1000 #edb/ref 17592186340388 13194139535259 true] [17592186340385 1000 #edb/ref 17592186340389 13194139535259 true] [17592186340385 1000 #edb/ref 17592186340390 13194139535259 true] [17592186340385 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045450 13194139535259 true] [17592186340385 1041 "selection" 13194139535259 true] [17592186340385 1104 #edb/ref 17592186340386 13194139535259 true] [17592186340385 1110 #uuid "95d3d35c-615b-5331-841e-ed31636d7701" 13194139535259 true] [17592186340386 1034 "II and III only" 13194139535259 true] [17592186340386 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139535259 true] [17592186340386 1111 #uuid "84a726a4-30dd-5575-acf0-50d0b20dde51" 13194139535259 true] [17592186340387 1034 "I and III only" 13194139535259 true] [17592186340387 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139535259 true] [17592186340387 1111 #uuid "9847fbb0-